# Judge rubric: <metric name>

Copy beside the judge prompt and version both together. A rubric change is a
new metric version; scores from different rubric versions are not comparable.

## Identity

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Metric name and rubric version | `<name>-v1` |
| Judge model alias and served snapshot | `<alias>` / `<recorded per run>` |
| Judge prompt name and version | `<prompt>` / `<version>` |
| Output type | Binary pass/fail, or ordinal 1 to 4 (no midpoint) |
| Owner and review date | `<role>` / `<YYYY-MM-DD>` |

## Question

One sentence the judge answers about one output, for example: "Is every
factual claim in the answer supported by the provided context?"

## Inputs given to the judge

- Task input: yes / no
- Candidate output: yes
- Reference answer: yes / no
- Retrieved context: yes / no
- Nothing else: no system prompt of the candidate, no model name, no run label.

## Scale

| Score | Criterion | Anchor example (synthetic) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Pass | All required properties hold | `<short example>` |
| Fail | Any required property is missing or wrong | `<short example>` |

Required properties (each independently checkable):

1. `<property>`
2. `<property>`

Automatic fail conditions: `<for example, unsupported claim, unsafe advice>`.

## Output contract

The judge returns JSON validated by a schema: `{"reasoning": str, "verdict":
"pass" | "fail"}`. Reasoning comes before the verdict. Invalid JSON is a judge
error, counted and reported, never silently scored.

## Bias controls

- [ ] Pairwise comparisons run in both orders; disagreement counts as a tie.
- [ ] Length is not a criterion; a length-matched control set is included.
- [ ] Judge model differs from the candidate model family where possible.
- [ ] Candidate identity, run label and model name are hidden from the judge.

## Calibration against human labels

| Item | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Labelled sample size and sampling method | `<n>`, stratified by `<tags>` |
| Labellers and instructions version | `<count>` / `<version>` |
| Inter-annotator agreement (Cohen's kappa) | `<value>` |
| Judge vs human agreement (kappa, true/false positive rates) | `<value>` |
| Minimum accepted agreement | `<value>` |
| Re-calibration trigger | judge, prompt or rubric change; drift in sampled audits |

The judge may gate releases only after it meets the minimum agreement on the
held-out labelled sample.
